# mgdoc.msg - Doc Church, Megaton Clinic (mgdoc.ssl).
# 100-109 look and examine, 110-119 floating lines, 200+ conversation.
# One entry per line; no braces in comment lines.

{100}{}{You see Doc Church, Megaton's only doctor.}
{101}{}{A lean man past fifty in a coat that was white under an earlier government. He looks at you the way a butcher looks at a cut of meat that is about to argue.}

{110}{}{Next.}
{111}{}{If it isn't bleeding, it can wait.}
{112}{}{Don't bleed on the clean table.}
{113}{}{Seven patients. One bottle of iodine.}

# Greetings: 200 first, 201 later, 202 colleague, 203 bomb disarmed, 204 Simms dead.
{200}{}{Rule one: don't waste my time. Rule two is the same rule. Hurt, sick or glowing?}
{201}{}{You again. Hurt, sick or glowing?}
{202}{}{You. Good. Somebody in this town who knows a scalpel from a spoon. What is it?}
{203}{}{The bomb tamer. You've cost me half my radiation trade. Don't apologize. Hurt, sick or glowing?}
{204}{}{I dug the bullet out of Simms afterwards. Force of habit. It didn't help him any. Hurt, sick or glowing?}

{210}{}{Patch me up.}
{211}{}{I've been near the bomb.}
{212}{}{Something bit me. I feel sick.}
{213}{}{Show me your supplies.}
{214}{}{What do you make of the Children of Atom?}
{215}{}{Neat sutures on that tray. Catgut, or did you find silk?}
{216}{}{Nothing more. I'm leaving.}
{217}{}{Owie.}
{218}{}{Me go.}

# Quotes are built as text + price + text.
{220}{}{Hold still. [He looks you over like a brahmin he has decided not to buy.] That will be $}
{221}{}{. Paid first. Dead men don't settle bills.}
{222}{}{Do it. [Pay him.]}
{223}{}{Too much. Forget it.}
{224}{}{[An hour of needle, thread and no conversation.] Done. Try to keep your insides inside. I don't give refunds.}
{225}{}{No money, no medicine. I'm a doctor, not a church. The church is the fellow standing in the puddle.}

{230}{}{Near it. Of course you were. The whole town lives downhill from that thing. Flushing you out takes two hours and most of my RadAway. $}
{231}{}{. You'll hate every minute of it.}
{232}{}{[Two hours of drips, cramps and a bucket.] There. You no longer tick. Stay out of the puddle, or don't. I charge either way.}

{235}{}{Show me the bite. Hm. You'll live, which is hard luck on whatever bit you. $}
{236}{}{. The antivenom tastes exactly as you'd expect.}
{237}{}{[He jabs you with something yellow and tapes over the hole.] Done. Next time, bite it back.}
# Asked to flush radiation out of a poisoned patient: the antidote comes first (238 + price + 239).
{238}{}{One thing at a time. Whatever bit you goes first, or two hours of flushing will finish what it started. The bite is $}
{239}{}{. Then we talk about the glow.}

{240}{}{Half my patients are his congregation. They stand in that puddle singing while their hair falls out, then walk up here and ask me why. I tell them. They go back. It's a slow suicide with hymns.}
{241}{}{And the bomb itself?}
# The bomb: 242 dormant, 243 disarmed, 244 rigged.
{242}{}{It has sat there for a hundred and sixty years, which is a better record than most of my patients. If you mean to poke at it, clear your head first. I sell Mentats. That is the entire prescription.}
{243}{}{Dead, I'm told. The puddle is still hot and the faithful are still in it. You cured the one thing in this town that wasn't hurting anybody.}
{244}{}{It hums now. I've had patients do that just before the end. If you know why it hums, undo it. If you don't, I would start walking.}

{250}{}{Catgut. I haven't seen silk since... [He actually looks at you.] You've held a needle. Fine. Colleague's rate: a quarter off. And if you plan to poke at that bomb, clear your head first. I keep Mentats.}
{251}{}{Then back to business.}

# INT 3 and below.
{260}{}{[He sighs like a tire going down.] Sit. Touch nothing. That will be $}
{261}{}{. Show me the coins. No. The round ones.}
{262}{}{Me pay. [Give him the coins.]}
{263}{}{No! Me keep shinies.}
{264}{}{Done. Go away. Don't eat the bandage.}
{265}{}{There is nothing wrong with you that I can fix. Go and stand somewhere else.}
# 267 to 269: what he is about to do, said before 260 (hurt, poisoned, glowing).
{267}{}{You leak. I sew. }
{268}{}{You are poisoned. I have the cure. }
{269}{}{You glow. I wash the glow out. It takes two hours. }
{266}{}{That isn't enough coins. Come back with more, or stop bleeding. Either will do.}

{270}{}{That's the lot. If it isn't on the shelf, the town has used it already.}
{271}{}{One more thing.}
{272}{}{What else? Make it quick. Somebody out there is busy getting hurt.}
